A||||6||rodmcneill||89 Fiero parts?||||5||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||12-09-2000||12:35 AM||||I have seen over the past year people selling various parts for 89 Fieros. These parts were supposed to be used on the new models but when production ended the parts were either destroyed or junked. Does anyone have any info. on these parts? What parts survived the dumpster? What 89 parts were at the factory waiting to be put on the models that we will never see?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fformula88||12-09-2000||12:38 AM||||I would be very suspicious of any new "89" parts for Fiero's.  The redesign prototype of the Fiero has been called the 89 Fiero, but it would not have gone into production until 1990.  So I wouldn't think any redesign parts had been manufactured yet and sent to the factory, or even exist.  Of course, thats just a hunch, I could be wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-09-2000||02:39 AM|||| <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=517581004" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=517581004</A> <P>Saw the above on Ebay. Seller states these are for the 1989 model run prior to the plant shutting down. I'd be curious about it since the 1988 dealer brochure lists power steering to be added in late 88 and the reason I have heard they never got put on was due to the announcement of the plant closing. So if they did't install a mid-year option why would they make or store 1989 parts? We all have seen the proposed 1989 body changes but those could have been designed before the closing announcement due to engineering time. (Bet the Ebay person really has left-over spare parts from the 1988 model run)<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||Standard||12-09-2000||03:05 AM||||From what I've heard, there were actually a limited number of 88's with power steering built, but as soon as the order was made to cancel it they stripped the power steering equipment off of them and sold the cars..<P>As for the "One of a Kind Fiero 1989 Front Rotor Assembly" I think the guy had a pair of 88 rotors sitting around for too long and he's trying to find a sucker... 500 bucks for brake rotors?!  bahahahahahahahaha<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 153,000 miles and some busted up ground f/x  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/redface.gif"> <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||007DOUG||12-09-2000||03:15 AM||||Don't know exactly what year to call the parts, but they are exactly what he says they are.<P>Seller is well known in fiero community. (HINT:Look at his email addy)<P>He's ebayed '89 end links, and the master VIN list for all the Indy pace cars produced. (I confirmed the list's authenticity, and tried like heck to win that one).<P>------------------<BR>5 Indy pace Cars<BR>84 T-top<BR>84 SE<BR>86 SE V6 153K<BR>86 GT<BR>86 GT now with a 4:10<BR>86 GT 3.4 Pearl White T-top<BR>2 88 GT's (twins)<P>However......<BR><I>"Time is my most precious and most perishable of all my possessions."</I>||||reg||14
Z||000005||Black88GT||12-09-2000||03:21 PM||||Yeah no offense but some of you guys tend to open your mouths a little too fast.<P>The seller is Joe Wynman, who has a bunch of super rare, even one of a kind Fiero parts (don't know how he got them).  If he says they were prototype 89 rotors, I would tend to believe him.    <P>Fformula88, how do you figure that they wouldn't have gone into production until 90?  Where did you get this info. from?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||12-09-2000||04:32 PM||||I am certain I read that information somewhere.... that the redesign was schedualed for 1990.  Now I cannot be certain of the source, but I am thinking it was out of the Gary Witzenburg book.  I had gooten the copy out of the library so I don't have it to confirm.<P>Are there a difference between those rotors and a standard 88 rotor?  Since the 88 braking system was new to the Fiero that year, I would doubt GM would go thru the trouble to engineer another rotor for the next model year.  They may have 89 part numbers if they were produced for the next year, but that shouldn't make the m a "special" part if they are not different.  But if they are different (thicker or larger in diameter) it would be a different story.<P>Orief, I heard there were a few prototype cars that had the power steering system on them, and it was indeed going to be a mid-year option for 88 until they decided to end production that year.||||reg||1
